10:10:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s understandable why that explanation is popular, but it’s an oversimplification. People with ADHD can absolutely become overstimulated—especially by noise, social demands, multitasking, stress, or lack of sleep—but ADHD is not fundamentally “an overstimulated brain.” It involves differences in regulation of attention, motivation, inhibition, and brain signaling systems such as dopamine and norepinephrine. Stimulants do not truly have an opposite effect in ADHD. At an appropriate dose, they can improve signaling in networks involved in executive function, especially the prefrontal cortex. That can make attention less scattered, reduce impulsive behavior, and make a person feel more settled. A person may look calmer because they can direct and regulate their attention better—not because the stimulant is sedating them. The same stimulant can still cause classic stimulant effects in someone with ADHD: faster heart rate, anxiety, jitteriness, irritability, insomnia, or increased hyperactivity, particularly at higher doses or in people who are caffeine-sensitive. Coffee is especially variable because caffeine is relatively weak, short-acting, and affected by sleep, food, tolerance, anxiety, and dose. Some people with ADHD feel focused and calmer after coffee; others feel noticeably more wired.
