10:10:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That description is real: many people with ADHD are visibly restless, fidgety, fast-moving, and mentally “on.” But those behaviors do not necessarily mean their nervous system is overstimulated in the clinical sense. “Overstimulation” usually means too much incoming demand or sensory/emotional input—such as loud noise, crowds, interruptions, bright lights, stress, or too many tasks—leading to overwhelm, irritability, shutdown, panic, or an inability to process more information. People with ADHD often do experience that. But fidgeting, inability to sit still, talking a lot, and constant movement can also be ways of regulating attention and arousal. Movement or small stimulation can help some ADHD brains stay engaged. For example, someone may tap their foot or pace during a conversation because it helps them listen, not because the conversation has overloaded them. So a person can be: - **under-engaged / seeking stimulation** → restless, fidgety, distracted, moving around to focus - **overstimulated / overloaded** → overwhelmed, irritable, panicky, shut down, unable to filter input - or sometimes both at once, depending on the setting A stimulant medication—or occasionally caffeine—can improve attention control and reduce impulsive movement in some people, which may look like “calming down.” But it does not mean the stimulant is acting as a sedative, nor that all ADHD restlessness is caused by overstimulation. Too much stimulant can absolutely push someone into genuine overstimulation and make the jitteriness worse.
