10:11:24 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Neither. People with ADHD are not wrong about feeling overloaded, mentally noisy, restless, or unable to filter competing input. Those are common and important ADHD experiences. The distinction is between: - **What the experience feels like:** “Everything is coming at me at once; my brain is too loud; I can’t tune things out.” Calling that overstimulation is reasonable. - **What ADHD is, biologically:** it is not established as one universal state of chronic global overstimulation. It is a heterogeneous neurodevelopmental condition involving difficulty regulating attention, inhibition, motivation, arousal, and sensory input. Different people—and the same person in different contexts—can be overloaded, under-engaged, stimulation-seeking, or both. The radio analogy works well: medication may help a person select and maintain one signal while suppressing competing signals. That can genuinely feel like turning down overstimulation. The technical nuance is only that stimulant medication is not believed to work by sedating the brain or simply lowering all stimulation. It tends to improve regulation and filtering in attention-control systems. The lived result may absolutely be “my mind is quieter.” So your psychiatrist’s framing and people’s self-reported experience can be valid. The disagreement is mainly over whether “ADHD is fundamentally chronic overstimulation” should be treated as a literal, universal biological explanation rather than a useful description of a very common experience.
